rked >):\n```\n 156                  capabilities:\n 157                    drop: [\"ALL\"]\n 158  ---\n 159  # Egress: DNS, the EHR MCP endpoint (private link), and the platform egress proxy only.\n 160  # The proxy allows api.anthropic.com, the payments and SMS providers, the eligibility agent,\n 161  # the group STS and auth service, and AWS service endpoints.\n 162  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\n 163  kind: NetworkPolicy\n 164  metadata:\n 165    name: clinic-agent-egress\n 166    namespace: clinic-agent\n 167  spec:\n 168>   podSelector: {}\n 169    policyTypes: [\"Egress\", \"Ingress\"]\n 170    ingress:\n 171      - from:\n 172          - namespaceSelector:\n 173              matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress }\n 174        ports:\n 175          - { port: 8080, protocol: TCP }\n 176    egress:\n 177      - to:\n 178          - namespaceSelector:\n 179              matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system }\n 180            podSelector:\n```\n\n## B08.2\n\n**Check:** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** infra/terraform/main.tf:69 — \"http_put_response_hop_limit = 1\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Egress limited to proxy/DNS/EHR /28, IMDSv2 required with hop limit 1, no hostNetwork/hostPath, private EKS endpoint. Credential breadth across tenants is filed under B02.\n\n`infra/terraform/main.tf` lines 57–81 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n  57      }\n  58    }\n  59  \n  60    eks_managed_node_groups = {\n  61      agents = {\n  62        instance_types = [\"m7g.large\"]\n  63        ami_type       = \"BOTTLEROCKET_ARM_64\"\n  64        min_size       = 2\n  65        max_size       = 6\n  66        desired_size   = 3\n  67        metadata_options = {\n  68          http_tokens                 = \"required\"\n  69>         http_put_response_hop_limit = 1\n  70        }\n  71      }\n  72    }\n  73  }\n  74  \n  75  module \"vpc\" {\n  76    source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\"\n  77    version = \"6.4.0\"\n  78  \n  79    name            = \"clinic-agent-prod\"\n  80    cidr            = \"10.42.0.0/16\"\n  81    azs             = [\"us-west-2a\", \"us-west-2b\", \"us-west-2c\"]\n```\n\n## B08.3\n\n**Check:** Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** k8s/deployment.yaml:162 — \"kind: NetworkPolicy\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Isolation is a NetworkPolicy plus namespace Pod Security labels, not prompt or harness code\n\n`k8s/deployment.yaml` lines 150–174 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n 150                  - name: GIT_SHA\n 151                    value: e4b7c19d2f80a3561c7e9b0d4f2a6c8e1b3d5f70\n 152                securityContext:\n 153                  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n 154                  privileged: false\n 155                  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n 156                  capabilities:\n 157                    drop: [\"ALL\"]\n 158  ---